# What the steam control law is given, rather than compiled with.
#
# This is the steam side's companion to tolerance.declaration: not properties
# of a casting, but the policy choices this design makes about the steam path
# -- where it holds it, how hard it leans on an error to keep it there, and how
# it sequences the heater and the feed pump when a draw begins -- read by the
# control law rather than compiled into it.
#
# The figures were chosen against thermoblock.params and against the behaviour
# asked of the loop, in host simulation. Nothing here has been on a bench, and
# neither have the coefficients it was chosen against. Every line says so.
#
# A note on the shape of the loop, because several figures below only make
# sense against it. While no draw is under way the loop drives the block's
# measured temperature to the ready target; the instant the wand's microswitch
# reports a draw, the variable it drives to becomes measured pressure, because
# a draw is exactly what parts pressure from the temperature that would
# otherwise imply it. Feed is held at nothing for the margin-building interval
# while the heater alone runs at its ceiling, then rises over the feed-rise
# interval to the sustainable rate and is never commanded past it.

# --- Where the design holds the steam path ----------------------------------

# The pressure the steam feed pump is withheld below outright.
#
# At the declared slope in thermoblock.params (steam.pressure_bar_per_k,
# 0.036 bar/K) and the declared saturation temperature (100 C), a steam block
# run at the temperature that file's own account describes -- "about a bar of
# gauge pressure at the temperature the steam block is run at" -- sits close
# to nine tenths of a bar above rest. Nine hundred milli-bar is taken from
# that same account rather than a fresh judgement, so the two figures cannot
# disagree about what "ready" means on this machine without both being
# revisited together. Nothing has been measured; the coefficients this is
# read against have not been either.
ready-pressure-bar = 900 milli-bar @estimated Read from thermoblock.params's own account of what gauge pressure a steam block run at its working temperature carries, at the declared saturation temperature and pressure-per-kelvin slope -- not a bench measurement, and not established against a real pressure transducer.

# The block temperature the loop holds while nothing is being drawn.
#
# It is the temperature whose rested pressure is the threshold above, read back
# through the same saturation relation those two figures already agree through:
# a hundred degrees of saturation plus nine hundred milli-bar at thirty-six
# milli-bar per kelvin is twenty-five degrees above it. Declaring it as a
# temperature rather than reusing the pressure figure is what makes the
# ready-holding phase drive to the block's own measured temperature, which is
# the quantity that is actually informative while nothing is being drawn --
# with the wand shut the two are tied, and the temperature channel is the one
# whose reading does not depend on a transducer nobody has fitted yet.
ready-temperature-c = 125000 milli-c @estimated Derived from ready-pressure-bar above through thermoblock.params's declared saturation temperature and pressure-per-kelvin slope, so the two cannot come to mean different states of the same machine without both being revisited. Nothing has been measured, and the slope it is read through is the description's own estimate.

# The two edges of the pressure band a draw is held inside.
#
# The floor sits deliberately above the ready threshold, and the gap between
# them is the whole reason the margin-building interval exists. Ready means the
# block has enough behind it that a draw is not starved; in band means the
# steam actually coming out has the character the drinker's milk needs. A
# machine sitting exactly at ready has the first and not yet the second, and
# what closes the distance is a couple of seconds of the element with nothing
# being drawn out of the block.
#
# The width between the two is what the loop has to work inside for the whole
# of a draw, at either end of the error thermoblock.params declares on the
# coefficient governing how far a draw drags the path down. It is not a
# statement about what a drinker can taste: no bench has established what band
# a steamed jug actually needs, and this is a band the design can hold rather
# than one the drink has asked for.
draw-pressure-floor-bar = 1000 milli-bar @estimated Chosen a hundred milli-bar above ready-pressure-bar, which is between one and two seconds of the steam element with nothing drawn out of the block at the declared thermal mass and rating -- so a draw beginning from a correctly held ready state reaches it inside the margin-building interval below with room to spare. It is not established that steam below this figure is wet, or that steam above it is not: no bench has produced either reading.
draw-pressure-ceiling-bar = 1400 milli-bar @estimated Chosen four hundred milli-bar above the floor, wide enough that the loop holds the whole span at either end of the declared error on steam.pressure_fall_bar_per_ml -- the loosest figure thermoblock.params carries -- across draws from a few seconds to a minute, and narrow enough to still refuse a path that has run away with the element left on. Nothing has been measured, and no bench has established what pressure a steamed jug is actually spoiled at.

# --- How the loop sequences a draw ------------------------------------------

# How long feed is held back after a draw begins.
#
# Steam made before the block has thermal margin to give is the wet, degrading
# start this loop exists to avoid, and leading with heat rather than with feed
# is what prevents it. Two seconds at the declared thermal mass and element
# rating carries a block sitting at the ready target roughly a hundred and
# seventy milli-bar upward with nothing being drawn out of it, which clears the
# floor above with margin.
margin-building-interval-ms = 2000 milli-s @estimated Estimated from thermoblock.params's declared steam thermal mass, element rating and loss, as rather more than the time the element alone needs to carry a block at the ready target above the band floor with nothing drawn out of it. It is not established that two seconds of margin is what dry steam actually needs on this machine: no bench has measured how a real block behaves at the first moment a wand is opened.

# How long engaged feed takes to reach its sustainable rate.
#
# A feed pump commanded from nothing to its settled rate in one step asks the
# block for the whole of a draw's load in the interval it is least able to
# answer it -- the moment the operator opened the wand, before any error has
# had time to appear for the loop to answer. Bringing it up over an interval is
# what lets the heater's own answer arrive alongside the load rather than
# behind it.
feed-rise-interval-ms = 1500 milli-s @estimated Estimated as somewhat under the margin-building interval above, so the rise is complete well inside the shortest draw anybody would open the wand for, and long enough at the loop's cadence to be many steps rather than a step or two. It is not established that a step change in feed would actually spoil the first of a draw on a real machine; what is established here is only what it does to this model.

# The level engaged, settled feed is commanded at, and never driven past.
#
# This is the figure that makes quantity yield before quality. There is no
# reading of how much steam the operator is asking for -- the microswitch says
# the wand is turned and nothing else -- so no law here could chase demand even
# if it wanted to, and this cap is what a draw beyond the block's capacity gets
# instead: less steam, at the character the band above states, rather than as
# much as was asked for at whatever character was left.
#
# It is deliberately well under the rate the element could boil at its ceiling.
# A rate at that point balances the block's energy and nothing else: the
# pressure a draw costs the path goes on accumulating with nothing left to
# raise the saturation relation against it, so the loop would have no authority
# left to hold the band with.
sustainable-feed-rate = 67 permille @estimated Estimated from thermoblock.params as a fifth of a millilitre a second -- sixty-seven permille of the declared 3 ml/s feed pump -- which is roughly three fifths of what the steam element could boil at its ceiling once the declared standing loss is paid. The remaining authority is what holds the band at the far end of the declared error on steam.pressure_fall_bar_per_ml, which is where the loop has least of it. Neither the pump's rate nor the element's rating has been measured, and the sag coefficient this is sized against is the loosest figure the description carries.

# --- How hard the loop leans on an error ------------------------------------

# The gains the ready-holding phase tracks its temperature target with. The
# error is in kelvin and the answer is in permille of the steam element.
ready-temperature-gain = 30000 milli-permille-per-k @estimated Estimated on the same footing the brew loop's own proportional gain is, and at the same figure: thirty permille per kelvin is thirty watts per kelvin against the declared element rating, against a casting of 360 J/K. The steam side is the simpler of the two loops here -- there is no transport lag between the mass and where the reading is taken, because the sensor is on the block -- so the phase this gain has to leave is not eaten by a conduction corner the way the brew side's is. Nothing here is a measurement.
ready-temperature-integral-gain = 350 milli-permille-per-k-s @estimated Estimated as an integral time well above the reciprocal of the crossover the gain above implies, so that removing the standing error costs little of the phase margin that gain was chosen to keep. It is the term that carries the block's standing loss, which is the whole of the load while nothing is being drawn and which no feedforward here answers -- see feed-load-gain below for why that division was drawn where it is. Nothing here is a measurement.

# The gains the draw phase tracks its pressure target with. The error is in bar
# and the answer is in permille of the steam element. The target itself is not
# declared: it is the middle of the band above, which is where a loop with
# equal room either side of it holds.
draw-pressure-gain = 3000000 milli-permille-per-bar @estimated Estimated by carrying the ready-holding gain above through thermoblock.params's declared pressure-per-kelvin slope, so that the same distance from target commands the same duty whichever variable the loop happens to be driving to: thirty permille per kelvin at thirty-six milli-bar per kelvin is a little over eight hundred permille per bar. It is taken at three thousand rather than there, because a draw is the disturbance this loop is least able to wait out and the pressure phase is the one the band is actually asserted in. Nothing here is a measurement.
draw-pressure-integral-gain = 50000 milli-permille-per-bar-s @estimated Estimated as an integral time of the same order as the ready-holding phase's, carried through the same slope: it trims what the feedforward's own assumptions leave behind rather than carrying the draw itself, which is the feedforward's job. It is deliberately slow, so that a draw beginning and ending does not leave accumulated intent behind it. Nothing here is a measurement.

# The duty fed forward for each permille of feed commanded.
#
# It answers a load the loop has been told about rather than one it has to
# discover: the feed pump's level is what the loop itself just commanded, and
# the energy the water it pushes in will take to reach saturation and turn is
# known at that moment rather than after the pressure has fallen. On a block
# holding no reservoir the fall arrives faster than any feedback term could
# answer it.
feed-load-gain = 7500 milli-permille-per-permille @estimated Estimated from thermoblock.params as the declared feed pump's full-scale rate times the energy a millilitre takes to reach the declared saturation temperature from the declared feed temperature and then turn, divided by the declared element rating and by full pump scale: three millilitres a second at about 2492 J/ml against a kilowatt is seven and a half permille of element per permille of pump. Every figure in it is one the description supplies and none has been measured. What it still assumes is the feed pump's rate-versus-level relation, which the description states as a single full-scale figure and which no measurement has established as linear.

# The duty fed forward for what the block gives up to the room, whatever else
# is happening.
#
# It is a flat duty rather than a figure per kelvin of rise, which is where
# this parts company with the brew loop's own standing term. That loop's target
# is something a caller names anywhere in the machine's working range, so a
# fixed duty there would be exact at one temperature and wrong by a growing
# amount everywhere else. This loop's target does not move: it holds one
# declared ready state and one declared band, some tens of kelvin apart, over
# which the declared standing loss moves by well under a tenth of the element.
# What that costs is exactness at the top of the band, which the integral gains
# above trim; what it buys is one figure rather than a coefficient plus the
# ambient temperature it would have to be measured from -- and the ambient a
# block loses to is not a channel the hardware seam reports, so a per-kelvin
# term here would have to assume one.
standing-load = 158 permille @estimated Estimated from thermoblock.params as the declared steam-block loss per kelvin times the rise of the ready target above the declared ambient, divided by the declared element rating: 1.5 W/K over a hundred and five kelvin against a kilowatt is a hundred and fifty-eight permille. Neither the loss figure nor the ambient has been measured; both are the description's own estimates, and the loss is among the wider fractions that file carries.
